By the middle or so of the middle game, there's relatively
little need for a pet, in most cases.  Typically by then
you have enough food (or even a ring of SD), so you can
afford to drag things to an altar to curse-test them.  Your
strength has increased and you typically have a bag
of holding, so you can carry a fair amount of stuff
until you get to an altar.  You've got decent armor
(mithril if not DSM, and an MC3 cloak of some kind)
and have acquired a decent quantity of hitpoints, so
you are no longer in grave danger of being killed by
mundane monsters that are generated randomly all
over the place, and the things that *can* threaten
you are relatively predictable (in terms of where
you're likely to find them).  Plus, you have a
blindfold and telepathy, so you can peek ahead
when in doubt.  You also usually have a decent
weapon (e.g., a longsword) and some skill in it,
so you can kill most things in a few turns.  So you
don't really need a pet to fight for you -- unless you
want to kill a peaceful human or something.

If you play as a female character, non-gray DSM
will ensure that any polymorph traps you encounter
will give you an opportunity to acquire pet baby
dragons, which are good enough for most mid-game
purposes (e.g., killing a priest for his robe).


